# How much should a firm produce?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why is the best output where marginal revenue equals marginal cost? Does any real firm think this way?"
next: {"title":"What do firms really maximise?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/114-what-do-firms-really-maximise.cast"}

## Cost plus a margin
use: firm_cost_curves
with:
    mode: monopoly
    show:
      - demand
      - mr
      - mc
    mark_mr_mc: true
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 160
      w: 600
      h: 500
sources:
    - id: hallhitch
      title: Price Theory and Business Behaviour
      authors: Hall and Hitch
      year: 1939
      finding: 'Most of 38 firms priced at full cost: average cost plus a margin.'
    - id: ipn
      title: What Firms' Surveys Tell Us about Price-Setting Behavior in the Euro Area
      authors: Fabiani et al.
      year: 2006
      finding: 54% of ~11,000 euro-area firms set price as a markup over costs; 27% follow competitors.

    scratch smoke work ["Smoked fish, a kilo","cost  $20","+ 50%","= $30"] x 810 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cue1939 "Hall & Hitch, 1939" #b5482e cites ["hallhitch"] x 830 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cuefc "average cost + margin" #3d3833 x 830 y 250 font_size 24 hidden true
    scale share cites ["ipn"] x 160 y 380 width 680 label "euro-area firms pricing at cost + markup" unit % min 0 max 100 value 54 ticks 5 hidden true
    text cuerivals "another 27%: follow rivals" #2f6b8f cites ["ipn"] x 500 y 250 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cuegap "theory ≠ practice?" #b5482e x 810 y 420 font_size 40 hidden true
    text cuetest "fixed margin: a rival changes nothing?" #2f6b8f x 760 y 440 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Cost plus a margin"

How much should a firm produce? The textbook answer fits in one picture.
    draw axes demand label_demand mr label_mr mc label_mc

B: Keep selling while the next unit adds more to revenue t (@draw mr_mc label_mr_mc mr_mc_guide q_star p_star label_p_star@)han to cost. Stop where MR meets MC, and read the price off demand.

    pause 0.3

Clean. Here's what bothers me, though (@fade axes demand label_demand mr label_mr mc label_mc mr_mc label_mr_mc mr_mc_guide q_star p_star label_p_star to 0.3@): has any real firm ever drawn this?

B: Ask the smokehouse, the only one in town, how it prices a kilo of smoked fish. Say, twenty dollars of costs...
    draw smoke_box smoke_line_1 smoke_line_2

...plus fifty percent. Thirty dollars. No demand curve, no MR in sight.
    draw smoke_line_3 smoke_line_4

    highlight smoke_line_4 effect underline

B: Hall and Hitch's thirty-eig (@draw cue1939 cuefc@)ht firms said the same back in 1939: average cost plus a margin.

    erase smoke

And if that margin never moved, a new rival (@draw cuetest@) would leave the price untouched. That's something we can check.

    erase cue1939 cuefc cuetest

    fade axes demand label_demand mr label_mr mc label_mc mr_mc label_mr_mc mr_mc_guide q_star p_star label_p_star to 0

Today's surveys are far bigger. Guess: of eleven thousand euro-area firms surveyed in 2003 and 2004, what share set price as a markup over cost?
    draw share

    ask "Click the line at your guess." on share store g default 20
        right: Fifty-four percent: close to your {g:0}.
        wrong: You said {g:0}: it was fifty-four percent.

B: Fifty-four percent. Another quarter  (@draw cuerivals@)simply follow their rivals' prices.

    pause 0.3

    erase share cuerivals

    fade axes demand label_demand mr label_mr mc label_mc mr_mc label_mr_mc mr_mc_guide q_star p_star label_p_star to 0.3

So the rule in the textbook and the rule i (@draw cuegap@)n the office look nothing alike. Either the theory is wrong, or firms follow it without saying so.

    pause 0.5

## As if
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: P = MC*x/(x - 1)
    params:
      MC: {value: 20, fixed: true}
      s: {value: 3, min: 1.5, max: 8, step: 0.1, label: sensitivity |ε|}
    editable:
      - s
    controls: panel
    show_equation: false
    drag: false
    x_range:
      - 1
      - 8
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 100
    x_label: sensitivity |ε|
    y_label: Best price P ($)
    marks:
      - {kind: hline, at: 20, label: MC = $20, id: mcline}
      - {kind: point, at: s, label: true, id: best}
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 960
      h: 520
    grid: false
sources:
    - id: lester
      title: Shortcomings of Marginal Analysis for Wage-Employment Problems
      authors: Lester
      year: 1946
      finding: Firms ranked cutting output and jobs last of five responses to higher costs.
    - id: machlup
      title: Marginal Analysis and Empirical Research
      authors: Machlup
      year: 1946
      finding: Firms act as if they computed margins, like a driver overtaking.
    - id: alchian
      title: Uncertainty, Evolution, and Economic Theory
      authors: Alchian
      year: 1950
      finding: Market selection favours firms that behave as if maximising profit.
    - id: friedman
      title: The Methodology of Positive Economics
      authors: Friedman
      year: 1953
      finding: Judge theories by predictions; the expert billiard player acts as if.

    scratch lest cites ["lester"] work ["Lester 1946: if costs rise...","1. work more efficiently","2. labour-saving machines","3. sell harder","4. change price or quality","5. cut output and jobs"] x 500 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true
    text mach "Machlup: firms act as if" #3d3833 cites ["machlup"] x 420 y 560 font_size 34 hidden true
    text roads "Alchian 1950: fuel on one road only" #2f6b8f cites ["alchian"] x 420 y 460 font_size 32 hidden true
    text weed "stray from profit → weeded out" #3d3833 cites ["alchian","friedman"] x 420 y 360 font_size 32 hidden true
    math rule tex "P = MC \\times \\frac{|\\varepsilon|}{|\\varepsilon| - 1}" size 34 x 560 y 560 hidden true
    text cp50 "cost + 50%" #b5482e x 470 y 380 font_size 30 hidden true
    text ferry "ferry fish: |ε| = 5" #2f6b8f x 680 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true
    text disguise "a moving margin = MR = MC" #b5482e x 600 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch math top-right work [{"tex":"MR = P\\,(1 - 1/|\\varepsilon|)"},{"tex":"P\\,(1 - 1/|\\varepsilon|) = MC"},{"tex":"P = MC \\times \\frac{|\\varepsilon|}{|\\varepsilon| - 1}"}] font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "As if"

B: That clash set off a famous quarrel. In 1946 Richard Lester asked American manufacturers what they would do if their labour costs rose relative to rivals.
    draw lest_box lest_line_1

B: Cutting ou (@highlight lest_line_6 effect underline@)tput and jobs, the textbook reply, came last of five. Workin (@highlight lest_line_2 effect circle@)g more efficiently came first.
    draw lest_line_2 lest_line_3 lest_line_4 lest_line_5 lest_line_6

    erase lest

Fritz Machlup's reply was his driver, who overta (@draw mach@)kes without computing: firms act as if they had.

B: Armen Alchian sharpened it in 1950. Send travellers out of Chicago (@draw roads@) on roads picked at random, with petrol stations on only one road.

The ones who arrive all took that road, and look as if they chose it. Firms that stray far from profit are w (@draw weed@)eeded out the same way; Friedman's billiard player made the point again.

    erase mach roads weed

And there's a way to make peace. Cost-plus can be MR equals MC, if the margin is the right one.
    draw axes x_label y_label x_ticks y_ticks mcline mcline_label

MR equals MC works out as a markup ove (@draw curve_0 rule@)r marginal cost, and its size depends on one thing: how price-sensitive the buyers are.

B: The smokehouse's extra kilo costs twenty dollars. If a one percent price rise loses i (@draw best best_label best_guides cp50@)t three percent of sales, the best price is thirty dollars: cost plus fifty percent.

    erase cp50

Now a mainland supermarket starts selling smoked fish off the ferry.
    draw ferry

Its buyers get choosier, say five instead of three, and the best price drops to twenty-five dollars.
    animate params.s.value 5 duration 3 ghost true

    erase ferry

    draw panel

B: Try it: slide the price sensitivity and watch the best price. Loyal buyers, fat margins; choosy buyers, thin ones.
    explore

    animate params.s.value 5 duration 0.5

So a rule of thumb is fine as long as the thumb moves: a margin that shrinks w (@draw disguise@)hen buyers have options is MR equals MC in disguise.

    pause 0.3

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see where that formula comes from?

@math

    erase mathq disguise

If a one percent price cut wins epsilon percent more sales, the next unit brings in its price less the cut on all the rest: MR is P times one minus one over epsilon.
    draw math_box math_line_1

The best output sets that equal to MC.
    draw math_line_2

Solve for P, and the markup appears. At three, P is one and a half times MC: cost plus fifty percent.
    draw math_line_3

    erase math

@after

    erase mathq

    pause 0.3

## Asking about prices
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Falling
      - Flat
      - Rising
    values:
      - 40.5
      - 48.4
      - 11
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    bar_colors: same
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 60
    y_label: Share of firms, GDP-weighted (%)
    x_label: Cost of each extra unit, as output rises
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 620
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: blinder98
      title: 'Asking About Prices: A New Approach to Understanding Price Stickiness'
      authors: Blinder, Canetti, Lebow and Rudd
      year: 1998
      finding: 200 US firms interviewed 1990-92; prices typically change about once a year.
    - id: blinder94
      title: 'On Sticky Prices: Academic Theories Meet the Real World'
      authors: Blinder
      year: 1994
      finding: Only 11% report rising marginal cost; 40.5% falling (sample drawn in proportion to value added, so shares estimate shares of output).
    - id: ipn
      title: What Firms' Surveys Tell Us about Price-Setting Behavior in the Euro Area
      authors: Fabiani et al.
      year: 2006
      finding: Variable markups dominate; more competition, less markup pricing.

    text who "200 US firms, 1990–92" #3d3833 cites ["blinder98"] x 500 y 470 font_size 40 hidden true
    text once "prices change ≈ once a year" #2f6b8f cites ["blinder98"] x 500 y 380 font_size 34 hidden true
    text mcq "\"marginal cost\"?" #b5482e x 500 y 290 font_size 40 hidden true
    text conf "MC or average cost?" #8f887c cites ["blinder94"] x 820 y 520 font_size 28 hidden true
    text flat_note "flat: unit cost = MC" #b5482e x 820 y 430 font_size 30 hidden true
    text vary "fiercer rivals → less cost-plus" #2f6b8f cites ["ipn"] x 820 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true
    text road "same goal, another road" #3d3833 x 820 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Asking about prices"

If the right margin can carry the th (@draw who@)eory, the next step is to ask price-setters properly.

B: Alan Blinder's team did, face to face, with two hundred US firms between 1990 and 1992, picked at random with big firms likelier to be drawn.
    highlight who effect underline

The first surprise: the typical fi (@draw once@)rm changes its main prices only about once a year.

B: The second: 'marginal co (@draw mcq@)st' meant little to them. Blinder wrote that the term is not in most businesspeople's vocabulary.

    erase who once mcq

So the team asked plainly: as you produce more, does each extra unit cost more, the same, or less?
    draw axes

    ask "Drag the 'Rising' bar to your guess." on bar_3 store g default 30 voice b
        intro: Textbook cost curves bend upward. Your guess: what share said extra units cost more?
        right: Eleven percent: close to your {g:0}.
        wrong: You said {g:0}: it was eleven percent.

Eleven percent, as a share of output (@draw bar_1 bar_2@). Four in ten said extra units get cheaper, and nearly half said the cost stays flat.

B: Blinder warned that many seeme (@draw conf@)d to confuse marginal with average cost. Even so, rising cost was rare.

    erase conf

And flat cost changes the story. If each extra unit costs the same, cost per unit i (@draw flat_note@)s marginal cost, so cost-plus really is a markup on MC.
    highlight bar_2

B: Does the margin move with the buyers? In the euro-area surveys it mostly does: where firms could say, variable markups beat  (@draw vary@)fixed ones, and the fiercer the competition, the fewer firms use cost-plus at all.

    erase flat_note vary

So the surveys don't show firms ignoring the marg (@draw road@)in. They show firms reaching it by another road.

    pause 0.5

## Money left on the table
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Spoken like a pricing manager who read Blinder.
    good: Most firms would be happy with that margin.
    poor: Even full cost needs a second look sometimes.
    none: 'No answer, no markup: a price setter''s nightmare.'
sources:
    - id: dg
      title: Uniform Pricing in U.S. Retail Chains
      authors: DellaVigna and Gentzkow
      year: 2019
      finding: Near-uniform prices across stores; median chain forgoes $16m a year.
    - id: blinder98
      title: 'Asking About Prices: A New Approach to Understanding Price Stickiness'
      authors: Blinder, Canetti, Lebow and Rudd
      year: 1998
      finding: Firms set prices with market power; most report flat or falling marginal cost.
    - id: ipn
      title: What Firms' Surveys Tell Us about Price-Setting Behavior in the Euro Area
      authors: Fabiani et al.
      year: 2006
      finding: Markup pricing dominates; markups vary with competition.

    icon s1 size 90 icon_look picture of store in shops hidden true
    text t1 "$2.49" below s1 gap 10 #b5482e font_size 30 hidden true
    icon s2 size 90 icon_look picture of store in shops hidden true
    text t2 "$2.49" below s2 gap 10 #b5482e font_size 30 hidden true
    icon s3 size 90 icon_look picture of store in shops hidden true
    text t3 "$2.49" below s3 gap 10 #b5482e font_size 30 hidden true
    icon s4 size 90 icon_look picture of store in shops hidden true
    text t4 "$2.49" below s4 gap 10 #b5482e font_size 30 hidden true
    icon s5 size 90 icon_look picture of store in shops hidden true
    text t5 "$2.49" below s5 gap 10 #b5482e font_size 30 hidden true
    text c1 "rich town" above s1 gap 10 #3d3833 font_size 24 hidden true
    text c2 "poor town" above s2 gap 10 #3d3833 font_size 24 hidden true
    text c4 "many rivals" above s4 gap 10 #3d3833 font_size 24 hidden true
    text c5 "no rival" above s5 gap 10 #3d3833 font_size 24 hidden true
    row shops cites ["dg"] gap 80 fit.h 130 fit.w 840 fit.x 80 fit.y 450 hidden true
    text lost "−$16m a year" #b5482e cites ["dg"] x 500 y 330 font_size 52 hidden true
    text why "inertia · brand image" #8f887c cites ["dg"] x 500 y 250 font_size 32 hidden true
    text claim1 "responds like MR = MC" #3d3833 cites ["blinder98","ipn"] x 330 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    text stamp1 "mostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 740 y 330 font_size 38 hidden true
    text claim2 "exactly, by selection" #3d3833 x 330 y 240 font_size 32 hidden true
    text stamp2 "contested" #b5482e x 740 y 240 font_size 38 hidden true
    text answer "MR = MC, reached by a moving margin" #b5482e x 500 y 520 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Money left on the table"

B: Here's my doubt, though. If the market weeds out bad margins, big firms shouldn't leave money on the table for long.
    draw s1 s2 s3 s4 s5 parallel true

Some do. Stefano DellaVigna and (@draw t1 t2 t3 t4 t5 parallel true@) Matthew Gentzkow studied US food, drugstore and mass-merchandise chains.

Most charge nearly the same price in every store: (@draw c1 c2@) rich town or poor, ma (@draw c4 c5@)ny rivals or none.

B: Their estimate: the median chain gives up sixteen  (@draw lost@)million dollars of profit a year, compared with prices tuned to each store.

People in the industry blame manag (@draw why@)erial inertia and worry about the brand's image. And the chains survive anyway.

B: So the market is a loose (@fade s1 s2 s3 s4 s5 t1 t2 t3 t4 t5 c1 c2 c4 c5 to 0.25@) filter: it removes the worst pricing, not every mistake.

    erase lost why

How sure are we? That firms respo (@draw claim1@)nd to costs and demand roughly the way MR equals MC predicts: mostl (@draw stamp1@)y agreed.

B: That they hit it exactly, or that competition forces them to: (@draw stamp2@) contested.
    draw claim2

    hide s1 s2 s3 s4 s5 t1 t2 t3 t4 t5 c1 c2 c4 c5

So how much should a firm produce? Where MR meets MC, and most (@draw answer@) firms get near it by cost plus a margin that bends with their buyers, not by drawing curves.

    pause 0.5

    quiz "A cinema sells tickets at cost plus 20% and popcorn at cost plus 400%. What fits MR = MC?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: No other seller: once inside, buyers are far less price-sensitive about popcorn, so the best markup is far higher.
        wrong: Think about where the buyer's alternatives are: here, no other seller inside.
        + Inside, popcorn buyers have no other seller
        * The cinema has lost track of popcorn costs
        * Popcorn costs more to make than a seat

    hide claim1 stamp1 claim2 stamp2 answer

    cards habits check each
        Fits MR = MC: Airport coffee dearer, January sales
        Money left on the table: Menu unchanged as food costs jump, Best-seller sold out by noon daily

    ask "Fits MR = MC, or money left? Sort each card." on habits store f stage own voice b
        right: All four: prices that follow buyers fit; frozen ones leave money.
        wrong: Ask whether the price follows what buyers will pay: here, airport coffee and January sales fit; the frozen menu and the sold-out best-seller leave money.

    erase habits

    show claim1 stamp1 claim2 stamp2 answer

    quiz "A motorway café's sandwich costs $4.00 to make and sells for $6.00. Ingredients push the cost to $4.40, and drivers are as price-sensitive as before. What does the markup rule say?" voice b
        right: $6.60: with the same price sensitivity, the best markup stays fifty percent of the new cost.
        wrong: With unchanged sensitivity, the markup stays the same percentage of cost: here, $6.60.
        + Charge $6.60: the same 50% margin
        * Keep $6.00
        * Raise the price by exactly 40 cents

B: Near it, not on it. And whether profit is all they are after is a question of its own.
    highlight answer effect underline

    pause 0.5

