    '|a||So a rule of thumb is fine as long as the thumb moves: a margin that shrinks when buyers have options is MR equals MC in disguise.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9960
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Want to see where that formula comes from? Show the math, or skip it?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4440
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1nqlkn9'
    '|a||If a one percent price cut wins epsilon percent more sales, the next unit brings in its price less the cut on all the rest: MR is P times one minus one over epsilon.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14080
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||The best output sets that equal to MC.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:97n0sa'
    '|a||Solve for P, and the markup appears. At three, P is one and a half times MC: cost plus fifty percent.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9120
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:lfn6er'
    '|a||If the right margin can carry the theory, the next step is to ask price-setters properly.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5640
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Alan Blinder''s team did, face to face, with two hundred US firms between 1990 and 1992, picked at random with big firms likelier to be drawn.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9480
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:e30ny4'
    '|a||The first surprise: the typical firm changes its main prices only about once a year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:jcuu87'
    '|b||The second: ''marginal cost'' meant little to them. Blinder wrote that the term is not in most businesspeople''s vocabulary.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8000
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:gkl8ul'
    '|a||So the team asked plainly: as you produce more, does each extra unit cost more, the same, or less?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7200
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:ha50r4'
    '|b||Textbook cost curves bend upward. Your guess: what share said extra units cost more? Drag the ''Rising'' bar to your guess.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9080
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:866qqf'
    '|a||Eleven percent, as a share of output. Four in ten said extra units get cheaper, and nearly half said the cost stays flat.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8560
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Blinder warned that many seemed to confuse marginal with average cost. Even so, rising cost was rare.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:15j80vr'
    '|a||And flat cost changes the story. If each extra unit costs the same, cost per unit is marginal cost, so cost-plus really is a markup on MC.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9320
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:ya8ckp'
    '|b||Does the margin move with the buyers? In the euro-area surveys it mostly does: where firms could say, variable markups beat fixed ones, and the fiercer the competition, the fewer firms use cost-plus at all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14360
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1ydd2b8'
    '|a||So the surveys don''t show firms ignoring the margin. They show firms reaching it by another road.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1i3h7ol'
    '|b||Here''s my doubt, though. If the market weeds out bad margins, big firms shouldn''t leave money on the table for long.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||Some do. Stefano DellaVigna and Matthew Gentzkow studied US food, drugstore and mass-merchandise chains.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7480
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:14jvmg6'
    '|a||Most charge nearly the same price in every store: rich town or poor, many rivals or none.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5960
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:126dy4m'
    '|b||Their estimate: the median chain gives up sixteen million dollars of profit a year, compared with prices tuned to each store.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8960
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:aqlgyf'
    '|a||People in the industry blame managerial inertia and worry about the brand''s image. And the chains survive anyway.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6920
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:kwje7l'
    '|b||So the market is a loose filter: it removes the worst pricing, not every mistake.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6000
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1fddfz8'
    '|a||How sure are we? That firms respond to costs and demand roughly the way MR equals MC predicts: mostly agreed.':
